{:id "0057-lab-webgpu-guard"
 :title "Guard the lab's WebGPU path: pin the vertex-buffer limit in a test, check real WebGPU pipelines in the browser check, fall back instead of going blank"
 ;; :lupa/fixes records realized consequence for Lupa's judge scoring: 0049
 ;; added two per-instance attributes, taking the glyph mesh to ten vertex
 ;; buffers; WebGPU allows eight, and on Ben's Safari (2026-11-17) the lab
 ;; showed its caret and no letters. The foreman hotfixed it (commit
 ;; 72c39d4 and the one after: four packed attributes, no normal, seven
 ;; buffers). This order makes the class of bug impossible to ship again.
 :lupa/fixes #{"0049-lab-channels"}
 :lupa/deps #{"0052-lab-dress"}
 :lupa/area :lab
 :lupa/risk :medium
 :goal "The lab drew nothing on WebGPU for a day and no check noticed:
        WebGL2 has no vertex-buffer limit, the gate's browser only ever
        ended up on WebGL2, and WebGPU reports the failure asynchronously
        so the start-up probe's try/catch missed it. Three guards: the
        buffer layout as data with a test that pins the limit; a browser
        check that runs three.js's real WebGPU path and inspects every
        pipeline it creates; and a start-up probe that sees asynchronous
        WebGPU errors and falls back to WebGL2 rather than a blank page."
 :scope ["src/halfahead/lab/instance.cljc" "test/halfahead/lab/instance_test.cljc"
         "src/halfahead/lab/render.cljs" "src/halfahead/lab/main.cljs"
         "public/lab/index.html" "e2e/lab.mjs" "docs/LAB.md" "README.md"]
 :design "What the foreman found, for reference. In headless Chromium
          launched with --enable-unsafe-webgpu, navigator.gpu exists with
          a software adapter (maxVertexBuffers 8). three.js 0.187's WebGPU
          backend fails there at GPUTexture.createView, because this
          Chromium rejects the newer 'swizzle' field three passes, and the
          lab's probe falls back to WebGL2. With an init script that drops
          'swizzle' from the descriptor before calling the real
          createView, three's WebGPU path runs: the lab reports backend
          WebGPU and creates its pipelines, so validation is real. (The
          software device is then lost, 'A valid external Instance
          reference no longer exists', and nothing is presented, so
          pixels cannot be checked there.) Wrapping GPUDevice.prototype
          .createRenderPipeline and createRenderPipelineAsync in the same
          init script records each descriptor's vertex.buffers: before the
          hotfix the glyph pipeline asked for 10 buffers and the console
          showed 'Render pipeline creation failed ... Vertex buffer count
          (10) exceeds the maximum number of vertex buffers (8)'; after
          it, 7 buffers and no such error.
          halfahead.lab.instance (.cljc, pure data): the glyph mesh's
          per-instance attributes, moved out of lab.render so a test can
          see them:
            attributes -> an ordered vector of {:name s :width n :holds
              [kw ...]}: glyphRect 4 (:rect); glyphLook 4 (:opacity :glow
              :wobble :ghost); glyphShape 3 (:weight :blur :fill);
              glyphInk 3 (:ink).
            (vertex-buffers) -> the buffers the glyph pipeline binds:
              position, uv, the instance matrix (count it always: three
              binds it as a buffer), and one per per-instance attribute:
              7. The geometry has no normal (the hotfix deletes it).
            (vertex-attribute-slots) -> 1 + 1 + 4 (a mat4 takes four) +
              one per per-instance attribute: 10.
            limits -> {:max-vertex-buffers 8 :max-vertex-attributes 16},
              WebGPU's guaranteed minimums.
          Tests: vertex-buffers <= 8 with at least one to spare, and
          vertex-attribute-slots <= 16; widths are 1 to 4; names are
          distinct. The docstring says a new channel goes into a packed
          attribute, never a new one, and why.
          lab.render: build the mesh's attributes from
          instance/attributes (no behaviour change; every existing pixel
          check passes unchanged).
          lab.render/start!: when the backend is WebGPU, take the
          GPUDevice from the renderer's backend, push error scopes
          ('validation', 'internal', 'out-of-memory'), draw the probe
          frame (it must contain at least one letter and the caret so
          every pipeline the lab uses is created: make it so), wait for
          the renderer's work to be submitted, pop the scopes; if any
          reports an error, or drawing throws, or the device is lost
          during the probe, warn in the console with the message and fall
          back to WebGL2 exactly as the existing catch does. Be
          defensive: if the device cannot be found, behave as today.
          After start, a GPUDevice 'uncapturederror' is logged once and
          kept: window.__lab.gpuErrors() -> the messages (at most 5), and
          the status label then reads 'Rendering with WebGPU, with errors:
          add ?webgl to the address'.
          lab.main: ?webgl in the URL starts the renderer on WebGL2
          directly. window.__lab.vertexBuffers() ->
          (instance/vertex-buffers).
          e2e/lab.mjs, a new step 'webgpu', in a second browser launched
          with --enable-unsafe-webgpu and the init script described
          above (the swizzle shim and the pipeline recorder):
            (a) open /lab/?reveal=3&debug and type a word. If the lab
            reports backend WebGPU: assert every recorded pipeline has at
            most 8 vertex buffers and 16 attributes, that the glyph
            pipeline (the one with the most buffers) has at most
            (instance/vertex-buffers), and that no console message
            contains 'pipeline creation failed' or 'exceeds the maximum';
            report the counts. If navigator.gpu is absent or the lab
            ended on WebGL2 anyway, say so in the step's line and assert
            only that the word is drawn (do not fail: other machines
            differ).
            (b) with the same browser and the shim, the lab must never be
            blank and silent: either letters are drawn (inked pixels in
            the word's box) or the status label names an error or WebGL2.
            In this container the software device is lost after start, so
            expect the fallback or the error label; assert one of the
            three outcomes and report which.
            (c) /lab/?webgl ends on WebGL2 and draws the word.
          Every existing step runs as now.
          docs/LAB.md: the limit, the packed attributes, how to add a
          channel, the WebGPU step and what it can and cannot prove,
          ?webgl. README.md: one line for ?webgl."
 :acceptance
 ["instance tests as in the design, on both hosts; `bb test` stays green"
  "`bb lab:e2e` passes with the webgpu step and every existing step
   unchanged (the specimen's pixel checks especially)"
  "The final message says plainly what was and was not verified on
   WebGPU, and what the lab does in this container when the software
   device is lost"]
 :non-goals
 ["No change to what is drawn, to the pure core, or to the schema"
  "Do not upgrade three.js or Playwright"
  "Do not run `bb e2e` or `bb play`"]
 :context ["src/halfahead/lab/render.cljs" "src/halfahead/lab/main.cljs"
           "public/lab/index.html" "e2e/lab.mjs" "docs/LAB.md" "README.md"
           "DESIGN.md"]
 :budget-usd 12.0}
